  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nBreezy winds will accompany a Pacific cold front moving in tandem\nwith an upper-level trough across the Rockies this afternoon.\nWinds could occasionally gust up to 40 mph (50 mph in the \nSacramento Mountains). Dust is not expected with these winds. \nSkies will range from mostly sunny to partly cloudy today as \ntemperatures fall to 2-8 degrees below normal.\n\nHigh pressure will build in tonight, and how much radiational \ncooling the desert lowlands of New Mexico (north of Cruces) could\nsee will hinge on how fast the winds diminish. Dewpoints dropping\ninto the upper-10s to lower-20s and a lack of cloud cover will \nhelp. The weakest gradient will be north of the I-10 corridor in \nNew Mexico. Upper-10s in the Gila cold spots are likely. The \nJornada Range and the Hatch Valley look like a fair bet for \nreaching the freezing mark, hence the Freeze Warning. The Mesilla \nValley is a 50/50 toss-up. We'll get another chance Sunday \nnight/Monday morning as winds will be lighter. \n\nBenign weather is favored early next week, though another passing\ntrough early Tuesday morning could produce a mix of wintry\nprecipitation in the northern Sacramento Mountains. Calm weather\nfor Wednesday, though the models are in disagreement on Thursday\nwith the pattern across the CONUS.",
